	"web_text": "Ere ibeji, which means 'sacred image of twins,' are figures made in Yoruba culture to remember twins who died as babies. These figures help the mother or surviving twin to keep caring for the spirits of the twins by washing, carrying, and giving them food. The carvings often have fancy hairstyles and decorations, like cowry shells, to show respect.",
